253 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af0ad9db-1ba6-5216-8e5f-5d768427a09e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065253 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1064 "What logarithmic equation is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nNotice that the given equation can be written using exponents as\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_{b}{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nwith $a = b^x$, we get\n\n$$\na = 2, b = 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_{4}{2}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1065 2780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "689eb94a-47df-5cd0-9019-d100d1621193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065351 #edb/ref 1067 "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1064 "A **logarithm** (or log) is an operation that's the *inverse* of exponentiation.\n\nFor example, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8\n$$\n\nBut we can also write this using a logarithm as\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nIn words, we would say \"log of $8$ with base ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ equals ${\\color{red}{3}}$\". The **base** of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\n\nSince exponents and logarithms are opposite operations, an exponential equation can always be expressed in logarithmic form, and vice versa.\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with positive base\n\n$$\nb \\neq 1\n$$\n\nis defined as follows:\n\n> $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1065 2863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4581c0d9-ad31-511c-a9d0-00d5ae0d5e38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065354 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1064 "The average rate of change of the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[a,a+h]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as the slope of the secant line that intersects the curve at the points $\\color{blue}P$ and ${\\color{red}Q}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3264f0792cee452453b024a61fb6f58a45290e133d3f84ec1ecf1c14501b4423.png)\n\nThe **instantaneous rate of change** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is the limit of the average rate of change as $h \\to 0$. It is denoted by $f'(a)$ and given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, as $h \\to 0$, the point $\\color{red}Q$ moves along the curve $y=f(x)$ towards the point ${\\color{blue}P}$, and the secant transforms into the line that touches the curve at exactly one point $({\\color{blue}P})$. That line is called the **tangent line** to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $\\color{blue}P$.\n\n![](images/37/3704b7e4f7a7aec6245d3494ef1e59a2b863e74a50bf5c9ecf471eec3789e7e9.png)\n\nThe value of $f'(a)$ is equal to the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1065 322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f6bb245-1f46-5ade-84d4-6fd172b4b227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way of defining $f'(a)$ is\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nWe arrive at this definition in the same way as before. But this time, we consider the interval $[a,x]$ instead of $[a,a+h]$, and then take the limit of the average rate of change as $x\\to a$.\n\n![](images/b4/b49c6bba1cb82227e7f47b2d1b91eae85ece94fd5251b79173842179cf7946e0.png)\n\nSometimes, this version is a bit easier to work with, so let's see an example of how to apply it. We should always arrive at the same answer regardless of which definition we use." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1065 324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6731f995-584b-5424-a320-c41c218307f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1067 "An Equivalent Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1064 "Derivatives naturally occur in many real-world situations, so it's essential to know their units of measurement. The key is to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}?$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ measures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1065 552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a148fb4-165b-500a-b7e6-a3c9c2776ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068809 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068809 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186