7592186074603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074603 #edb/ref 1061 154983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b327d3b-e449-5f4a-9b7f-318819c5309f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074604 #edb/ref 1061 154984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "039589a4-f68f-5959-bc9d-32aaa65805e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074720 #edb/ref 1061 29494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2480a381-e1e9-5ddf-97c9-5065c97a8cda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074923 #edb/ref 1061 9647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ec1bc50-2134-5ed0-aa3d-e29348fd99e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074924 #edb/ref 1061 9655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8d1a4d2-47c1-5676-a5b1-d16b02e3ee67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074925 #edb/ref 1061 9670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3698100e-df7d-5666-8520-aa605b0f8eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074984 #edb/ref 1064 "Any transformation that maps a polygon to a similar polygon is called a **similarity transformation**.\n\nAny combination of transformations made up of translation, rotation, reflection, and dilation will result in a similarity transformation.\n\nTo understand why this is true, remember:\n\n- Rigid motions (rotation, reflection, and translation) do not change the lengths or angles of the original figure. They map polygons to congruent polygons. If two polygons are congruent, then they are similar.\n- A dilation with scale factor $k$ multiplies all the lengths of the original shape by $k$ (leaving the angle measures unchanged), thereby producing a similar shape." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074984 #edb/ref 1065 2315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074984 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "622763c1-840b-5946-a1e7-feb59d8f0c89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074984 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074992 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the right triangle below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd056cb2c92d918412778089c0744525fb676a79047b0e779161c68db8210d54.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin A = \\frac{BC}{AC}, & \\cos C = \\frac{BC}{AC}, \\\\ \\cos A = \\frac{AB}{AC}, & \\sin C = \\frac{AB}{AC}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\cos C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\sin C\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nm\\angle A =\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle C$ are complementary, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 90^\\circ - \\theta\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ) \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement, and\n- the cosine of an angle equals the sine of its complement.\n\nSine and cosine are known as **cofunctions**. The latter is called **co**sine to indicate that it is the **co**function of sine." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074992 #edb/ref 1065 2456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074992 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b432c9c-d757-5975-9b24-999b2bce5a31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074992 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075000 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the above properties, we can also derive cofunction properties for secant and cosecant. We start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\frac{1}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\sin (90^{∘} - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the same reasoning, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have deduced that secant and **co**secant are also cofunctions. To summarize:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by similar reasoning, it's possible to show that tangent and **co**tangent are cofunctions. Their cofunction relationships are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\cot (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cot θ &= \\tan (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075000 #edb/ref 1065 2562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075000 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "04166248-ef12-5aa7-b46b-1a077920d60c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075000 #edb/ref 1067 "Reciprocal Functions of Complementary Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075050 #edb/ref 1064 "In the coordinate plane, the **midpoint** of a line segment with endpoints $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2,y_2)$ lies halfway between the $x$-coordinates $x_1$ and $x_2$, and halfway between the $y$-coordinates $y_1$ and $y_2$.\n\nThe coordinates of the midpoint can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n(x_m,y_m) = \\left(\\dfrac{x_1 +x_2}2,\\dfrac{y_1+y_2}2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, the coordinates of the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints $(2,1)$ and $(6,13)$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2 + 6}{2}, \\frac{1 + 13}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{2}, \\frac{14}{2}) \\\\\n&= (4, 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075050 #edb/ref 1065 8897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075050 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "164a0bd6-c234-5906-82c5-f00aefe19504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075050 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075051 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $M(4,2)$ is the midpoint between $A(1,1)$ and $B(a,b)$. What are the values of $a$ and $b$?\n\n![](images/d9/d946b98f81c7f30ff5a277c337be47dc743de531e3b138795d4a15f34ad82d54.png)\n\nAgain, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} &= 1, & y_{1}|= 1, \\\\ x_{2}|= a, & y_{2}|= b, \\\\ x_{m}|= 4, & y_{m}|= 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4, 2) &= (\\frac{1 + a}{2}, \\frac{1 + b}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the $x$-coordinates equal to each other and the $y$-coordinates equal to each other, we have the following two equations:\n\n$$\n{4 = \\frac{1 + a}{2}; 2 = \\frac{1 + b}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equations by $2$, we have:\n\n$$\n{8 = 1 + a; 4 = 1 + b\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve both equations independently:\n\n$$\n{a = 8 - 1 = 7; b = 4 - 1 = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7$ and $b=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075051 #edb/ref 1065 8900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "34ecfb6d-a13d-5bc2-b346-787701d55aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075051 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Coordinates of Endpoints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078403 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078403 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078403 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a19695af-0879-5a00-b400-1c4a490f7bfa" #edb/ref 17